# Optional GitHub Adapter Guide

> Inactive example only. This file is not `.github/` configuration, grants no
> permission, and does not assert that GitHub, a repository, credentials,
> reviewers, checks, branch protection, merge methods, environments, releases,
> or any other hosted setting exists.

The core small-team workflows are provider-neutral. A project may map their
hosted-change operations to GitHub only through a project-owned accepted
decision and current authority for each external action. Structural adoption
does not authorize a push, pull request, review, merge, remote branch deletion,
release, or settings change.

## Adoption inputs

Before adopting a GitHub mapping, record project-owned values for:

- the exact repository and default branch;
- the selected base workflow and optional `concurrent_work` modifier;
- whether branch publication and PR creation are authorized for the current
  task;
- the CI workflow and check names actually observed after configuration;
- established independent-review capacity and current eligibility;
- exactly one integration method;
- local and remote branch-cleanup rules; and
- evidence time, freshness deadline, limitations, and accepted decision ref.

Do not infer any value from this example. A null, unknown, stale, or
conflicting adoption input remains unresolved.

## Provider mapping

| Provider-neutral operation | Example GitHub behavior |
|---|---|
| `locate_pull_request` | Locate at most one PR for exact repository, base, head, and head revision; ambiguity or mismatch blocks. |
| `observe_change_checks` | Read the exact PR or commit checks without claiming they are required or successful. |
| `observe_pull_request_reviews` | Read review evidence for the exact PR and head; distinguish author, self-review, and an eligible other developer. |
| `open_pull_request` | Open a PR for exact base/head refs after separately authorized branch publication. |
| `submit_pull_request_review` | Submit an exact review while stating self versus peer review truthfully. |
| `merge_pull_request` | Merge the exact PR with the one adopted method only after current validation, review, and merge authority are established. |

These mappings are examples, not adopted settings. GitHub branch protection,
rulesets, required checks, merge-method availability, automatic branch
deletion, and permissions live at the hosted enforcement boundary and must be
verified independently. Repository files can recommend restrictions but
cannot create or prove those controls.

The governed work-completion engine uses `gh` only as the optional adapter for
these hosted observations and actions. It does not derive project authority
from `gh` configuration or authentication. Planning never invokes `gh`.
Apply requires an exact current authorization attestation before the first
provider call and rechecks its validity before each later call. The adapter
locates before it creates, re-observes after it acts, and records identifiers
and revisions so `resume` can recognize a previously completed action.

## Lightweight small-team guidance

- `solo_direct` does not require a PR. If GitHub is used, CI may run after the
  authorized default-branch push.
- `solo_hybrid` may use a self-PR. Record self-review limitations outside any
  implication of independent approval.
- `pair_pr` and `tiny_pr` require a PR and block when required review capacity
  is absent. Configure eligible provider reviewer identities explicitly and
  require an approval by one of them, distinct from the author. Never add more
  than one required peer approval.
- Use lightweight path ownership only when project evidence shows it improves
  reviewer selection; do not generate a CODEOWNERS hierarchy.
- If CI runs on both branch pushes and PR events, restrict ordinary push
  validation to the default branch so an open PR update does not run the same
  full suite twice.
- Concurrency may cancel superseded PR runs, but should preserve required
  default-branch and release evidence.

Copying an example into `.github/` is a separate repository mutation. Applying
branch protection, rulesets, merge settings, or any other hosted setting is a
separate external mutation. Both require explicit current authority and
project-specific validation.
